@prefix pmt:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/traditions/> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix pmw:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/works/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — Haṭhayoga
#
# Held separately from the Śaiva and Śākta material it draws on, because the
# Haṭhayogapradīpikā is a fifteenth-century manual with its own ordering and
# is the clearest case in the collection of a text that states its
# prerequisites in the base layer rather than leaving them to commentary.
#

#
# The works
#

pmw:Hathapradipika a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā"@en, "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā"@de ;
    skos:note "Every node of this file entered before 2026-10-22 cites the Sanskrit vulgate in the numbering of Sinh's translation and was read there; those citations are left as they stand. The nodes entered on 2026-10-22 were read in the Sanskrit of the Bombay printing with Brahmānanda's commentary, archive.org dli.ernet.429198, a text layer of 226402 Devanagari characters against none Latin. Its title page is not legible in the text layer; the colophon at the end of the commentary is, and gives the printing: at Bombay, in the Tattvavivecaka press, printed for Tukārām Tātyā, in Śaka 1814 and Vikrama 1949, on the tenth of the dark half of Māgha. The running heads of the Sanskrit are not monotonic in the text layer, so the Sanskrit is cited by chapter and verse without page."@en ;
    skos:note "Two translations by two translators, both read at every verse entered on 2026-10-22 except III.84-85, which Iyangar omits. Pancham Sinh, The Hatha Yoga Pradipika, translated into English (Allahabad: Sudhindra Nath Vasu, Bhuvaneshwari Ashrama), archive.org dli.csl.7087, title page in the text layer, year not legible there. Srinivasa Iyangar, Hatha-yoga-pradipika of Svatmarama Svamin, Part I, third edition (Adyar: Theosophical Publishing House, 1949; first edition 1893, second 1933), archive.org in.ernet.dli.2015.90055, title page in the text layer; it translates the verses and carries much of the Jyotsnā in its notes without always saying which sentence is the commentary's. Sinh numbers the first chapter from verse 17 two higher than the Bombay printing and Iyangar."@en ;
    skos:note "Read on 2026-10-22: I.11-17, I.56-64, II.10-14 and III.83-85, continuously, in the Sanskrit with the commentary and in both translations; besides that, a whole-text term search of the Sanskrit for the hermitage and for the terms of absence and incapacity (pmp:WaiverHathapradipikaMathaSearch). The earlier nodes rest on the verses they cite, and no record says how much of the work around them was read; chapter IV carries no node of this file."@en .

pmw:HathapradipikaJyotsna a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Brahmānanda, Jyotsnā, commentary on the Haṭhayogapradīpikā"@en, "Brahmānanda, Jyotsnā, Kommentar zur Haṭhayogapradīpikā"@de ;
    skos:note "Read on 2026-10-22 at the verses named under pmw:Hathapradipika, in the same Bombay printing, which names the commentary and its author in the colophons of the chapters. A later station of the same transmission and held as a work of its own, so that a release the commentary spells out is never read as the verse's. It states its own rule for the base text at I.12: verses of the Pradīpikā that it does not explain are to be taken as interpolated."@en .

#
# The tradition
#

pmt:Hathayoga a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Haṭhayoga"@en, "Haṭhayoga"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Haṭhayogapradīpikā of Svātmārāma and the manuals around it."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. Every verse cited in this file was read in the Sanskrit of the widely circulated vulgate, in the recension that the 1915 edition and translation of Sinh carries, and each citation now says so. The critical edition drawn from the manuscript tradition numbers differently in places; pmc:HathapradipikaRecensionDispute holds what is at issue."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:Hathapradipika, pmw:HathapradipikaJyotsna ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:Asana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Posture"@en, "Sitzhaltung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The seat, treated in the first chapter as the first limb of the practice."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:NadiPurification a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Purification of the channels"@en, "Reinigung der Kanäle"@de ;
    skos:definition "The clearing of impurity from the channels, at which the breath practice is said to aim, and whose accomplishment the text says shows outwardly in the body."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.6 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)", "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.19-20 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmp:HathaPranayama a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Breath regulation in Haṭhayoga"@en, "Atemregulierung im Haṭhayoga"@de ;
    skos:definition "The breath practice of the second chapter, directed at the clearing of the central channel."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:PranayamaPresupposesAsana a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Breath regulation presupposes firmness in posture"@en, "Atemregulierung setzt Festigkeit in der Haltung voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HathaPranayama ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Asana ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    skos:note "The verse opens the breath chapter by requiring that the practitioner first be established in posture and in a measured diet, and that the breath practice then follow the instruction of a teacher. The condition is in the base text, not in a commentary."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.1 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:SuccessPresupposesNadiPurification a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Success in the practice presupposes purification of the channels"@en, "Erfolg der Praxis setzt die Reinigung der Kanäle voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HathaPranayama ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:NadiPurification ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    skos:note "The text directs the breath practice at clearing the impurity of the central channel and gives the outward signs by which accomplished purification is known. It makes success depend on the purification without stating in these verses what befalls one who proceeds without it, which is why the strength here is order and not necessity."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.6 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)", "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.19-20 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:HathaChapterOrder a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "The manual orders posture, breath, seal and absorption"@en, "Das Handbuch ordnet Haltung, Atem, Siegel und Versenkung"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:NadiPurification ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Asana ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:presentationOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    skos:note "Recorded at the weakest strength on purpose. The four chapters run posture, breath, seal, absorption, and that arrangement is often cited as though it were itself a rule. The rule is in II.1; the chapter order is only an arrangement."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I-IV (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

#
# Recension and edition
#

pmc:HathapradipikaRecensionDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "The text and the verse numbering of the manual are edition-dependent"@en, "Textbestand und Verszählung des Handbuchs hängen von der Ausgabe ab"@de ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement among editors about what belongs to the manual and how its verses are numbered. The widely circulated editions descend from single manuscripts and differ in the count and extent of their chapters; a critical edition drawn from more than two hundred manuscripts, with the oldest dated 1496, settles neither question in favour of the numbering that most citations use."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a node because every citation in this file carries a verse number, and a number is only as stable as the recension it comes from. The numbering used throughout this file follows the widely circulated vulgate text; a reader working from the critical edition may find the same passage under a different number. Naming the manual without naming the edition conceals that anything is at issue."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmt:Hathayoga ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā, ed. and tr. P. Sinh (1915)", "Svātmārāma, Haṭhapradīpikā, critical edition, Kaivalyadhama S.M.Y.M. Samiti, Lonavla", "Svātmārāma, Haṭhapradīpikā, critical edition, ed. J. Mallinson, J. Birch, J. Hanneder, M. Demoto and N.-J. Liersch (2024)" .

#
# Which opening the breath takes
#

pmp:Ujjayi a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Ujjāyī"@en, "Ujjāyī"@de ;
    skos:definition "A retention in which the mouth is held closed and the breath is drawn through both nostrils so that it grazes the throat and sounds as it passes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.51-53 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmp:Sitkari a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Sītkārī"@en, "Sītkārī"@de ;
    skos:definition "A retention in which the breath is drawn in through the mouth with the tongue between the lips, producing a hissing sound, and is not let out the same way."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.54-56 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmp:Sitali a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Śītalī"@en, "Śītalī"@de ;
    skos:definition "A retention in which the breath is drawn in over the tongue, protruded past the lips, and released through the nostrils."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.57-58 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:CoolingAndQuenching a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Cooling and the quenching of hunger and thirst"@en, "Kühlung und das Stillen von Hunger und Durst"@de ;
    skos:definition "The state ascribed to the cooling retention: fever, bile, hunger, thirst and the effect of poisons are said to be put down."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.58 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:SitaliYieldsCooling a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Inhalation over the tongue yields cooling"@en, "Die Einatmung über die Zunge bringt Kühlung hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Sitali ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:CoolingAndQuenching ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    skos:note "Recorded because the opening through which the breath is taken is not incidental here but is what the practice consists in. Two of the eight retentions the manual lists take the breath in through the mouth and give it out through the nose; two others require the mouth held shut. The manual therefore assigns the opening per practice and does not set nasal breathing as a general rule, still less mark mouth inhalation as an error. See prima-materia#26."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.57-58 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:ForcedBreathCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Breath forced rather than tamed by degrees kills the practitioner"@en, "Ein erzwungener statt allmählich gezähmter Atem tötet den Übenden"@de ;
    skos:definition "A warning that the breath is to be mastered by degrees as a lion, an elephant or a tiger is mastered, and that it destroys the one who proceeds otherwise; performed wrongly, the practice is said to generate the very ailments it removes when performed rightly."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HathaPranayama ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    skos:note "The named consequences are hiccough, laboured breathing, cough, and pain in head, ears and eyes. This is the strongest thing the manual says about the manner of breathing, and it concerns pace and force, not the opening the breath passes through. See prima-materia#26."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.15 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)", "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.16-17 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

#
# What carries the practice: the pause, not the direction
#

pmc:KevalaKumbhaka a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Retention alone"@en, "Alleiniges Anhalten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Retention held at will without the filling and the emptying that ordinarily frame it. The manual calls it the accomplished form of the practice and says nothing in the three worlds is beyond one who holds it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.71-74 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:KevalaPresupposesSahita a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Retention alone presupposes retention with filling and emptying"@en, "Das alleinige Anhalten setzt das Anhalten mit Füllen und Leeren voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:KevalaKumbhaka ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:HathaPranayama ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    skos:note "The instruction is to keep practising the accompanied form until the unaccompanied one succeeds. Recorded at prescribed order because the verse sets a sequence without saying what befalls one who skips it. The direction of the breath drops out at the top of this sequence, which is what makes the claim that inhalation draws and exhalation expels a claim about the lower rungs only. See prima-materia#27."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.72 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:OpposedDirectionsOfPranaAndApana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "The two winds are driven in opposite directions at once"@en, "Die beiden Winde werden zugleich in entgegengesetzte Richtungen getrieben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The apāna is raised from below while the prāṇa is pressed down from the throat, and the two are brought together in the region of the navel."@en ;
    skos:note "The manual assigns direction to the two winds, not to the two phases of breathing. Both directions run in the same operation and against each other, so a scheme that reads inhalation as upward and exhalation as downward, or the reverse, has no purchase here. See prima-materia#27 and prima-materia#28."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.47 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)", "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.50 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

#
# Where the seat is upright and where it is not
#

pmc:ErectTrunkInSwastikasana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "The straight trunk as the mark of a seat"@en, "Der gerade Rumpf als Merkmal einer Sitzhaltung"@de ;
    skos:definition "A seat defined by sitting evenly with the body straight; the straightness is part of what the posture is, not a separate instruction."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.21 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:ChinToChestInTheChiefSeats a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "The chin pressed to the chest in the two seats the manual prefers"@en, "Das an die Brust gedrückte Kinn in den beiden bevorzugten Sitzhaltungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the two seats the manual singles out as chief, the chin is set firmly against the chest and the gaze is fixed between the brows or at the tip of the nose."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the counterweight to any claim that the manual requires an erect spine throughout. The seats it recommends most warmly close the neck rather than extend it, and the same posture chapter has the tongue set against the roots of the upper teeth while the wind is drawn upward. Uprightness of the trunk is attested; uprightness carried through the neck and head is contradicted in the same work. See prima-materia#29."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.37 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)", "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.46 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)", "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.48 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:BhastrikaPresupposesLevelNeckAndTrunk a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "The bellows breath presupposes neck and trunk held level"@en, "Der Blasebalgatem setzt einen gerade gehaltenen Hals und Rumpf voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HathaPranayama ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ErectTrunkInSwastikasana ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    skos:note "The verse binds the lotus seat, a level neck and trunk, and a carefully closed mouth into the one instruction that opens the bellows breath. It is the only place in the breath chapter where the alignment of the spine is stated as a condition of a breath practice, and it is stated for one practice, not for the chapter. See prima-materia#29."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.60 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

#
# The seals that set direction
#

pmp:Mulabandha a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "The root seal"@en, "Das Wurzelsiegel"@de ;
    skos:definition "A seal made by pressing the perineum with the heel and contracting the anus, by which the downward wind is drawn upward."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.61-63 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:ApanaForcedAgainstItsCourse a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "The downward wind driven upward by force"@en, "Der abwärts gerichtete Wind wird mit Gewalt aufwärts getrieben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The wind whose course is downward is said to be made to go upward by force; the manual states the natural course and the reversal in the same verse."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the direct reversal of any scheme that assigns downward carriage to an operation in the lower abdomen. The manual has the abdominal operation carry upward, and has the operation at the throat press the other wind down. Both assignments are the opposite of the one the modern draft makes. See prima-materia#28."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.62 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:KindledInnerFire a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "The kindled inner fire"@en, "Das entfachte innere Feuer"@de ;
    skos:definition "A heat said to arise when the raised wind reaches the region of fire, lengthening its flame, until the sleeping power is warmed through and straightens."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.66-68 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:MulabandhaYieldsInnerFire a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "The root seal yields the kindled inner fire"@en, "Das Wurzelsiegel bringt das entfachte innere Feuer hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Mulabandha ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:KindledInnerFire ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    skos:note "The pairing is set at the fire and not at the reversal on purpose. The manual defines the root seal as the drawing upward of the downward wind, so a claim that the seal produces that upward movement would assert only that the practice is what it is. The heat, the lengthened flame and the waking of the power are consequences the verses state beyond the definition, and they are what a protocol could address."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.66-68 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmp:Khecari a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Khecarī"@en, "Khecarī"@de ;
    skos:definition "A seal in which the tongue is turned back and set above the palate while the gaze rests between the brows; the manual describes a months-long preparation of the tongue before the position can be held."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.32-33 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)", "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.37 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:RetentionOfTheDescendingNectar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "The retention of the descending nectar"@en, "Das Zurückhalten des herabfließenden Nektars"@de ;
    skos:definition "The stopping of a fluid held to fall from above and to be consumed below, whose loss the manual makes responsible for ageing and death."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.51-52 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)", "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.77 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:KhecariYieldsRetention a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "The tongue set above the palate yields the retention of the nectar"@en, "Die über den Gaumen gelegte Zunge bringt das Zurückhalten des Nektars hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Khecari ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:RetentionOfTheDescendingNectar ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    skos:note "The contact at the palate is attested, and what it is said to do is stop a descent. It is not bound to a phase of breathing anywhere in the chapter, and it is not said to carry anything upward. The same holds for the seal at the throat, which is described as closing off a downward flow. A scheme that pairs the palate with inhalation and with upward carriage takes a stopping and reports it as a conveyance. See prima-materia#28."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.42 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)", "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.51-52 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

#
# How many stations the path has
#

pmc:SixteenSupports a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "The sixteen supports"@en, "Die sechzehn Stützen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A set of sixteen named stations of the body, called supports, which the seal at the throat is said to bind; the manual names the number in a compound and treats the binding of all sixteen as one operation."@en ;
    skos:note "Recorded because the number is the whole of the finding. A modern draft gives thirteen segments as the path along which the force is led; sixteen is what the base text names here, and other works of the wider corpus give eighteen. The count therefore varies across the transmission, which means there is no single path to be had, and a scheme that presents one number as the path has chosen a branch without saying so. The other witnesses to the sixteen and the eighteen have not been read at their wording and are not entered here. See prima-materia#35."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.73 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

#
# Placement on the awareness axes.
#

pmc:KevalaKumbhakaIsByEffort a pm:Situating ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Retention alone is attained by effort"@en, "Das alleinige Anhalten wird durch Anstrengung erlangt"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:KevalaKumbhaka ;
    pm:axisValue pm:byEffort ;
    pm:situationGround "The verse directs that the accompanied retention be practised for as long as it takes until the unaccompanied one succeeds, and the next verse names the condition by the success: it is called retention alone once it can be done without the emptying and the filling. The manual gives no other way to it, and the counted regimen of the same chapter sets a number of retentions to be performed at four times of the day."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.72-73 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:KevalaKumbhakaHoldsOwnAgency a pm:Situating ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Retention alone holds its own agency"@en, "Das alleinige Anhalten behält die eigene Trägerschaft"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:KevalaKumbhaka ;
    pm:axisValue pm:ownAgency ;
    pm:situationGround "The verse has the practitioner able to hold the breath at his own pleasure, and what follows is credited to that holding. Nobody else acts in these verses: there is no descent, no possession and no conferral, only a capacity said to be at the practitioner's disposal once the practice has taken."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.74 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:KevalaKumbhakaLocusNotStated a pm:Situating ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Retention alone states no locus"@en, "Das alleinige Anhalten gibt keinen Ort an"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:KevalaKumbhaka ;
    pm:axisValue pm:locusNotStated ;
    pm:situationGround "The verses were read for this question and are silent on it. They describe the breath and what the practitioner can do with it, and say nothing about where the awareness is held to be; the seated posture the chapter requires places the body and not the awareness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.71-74 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

pmc:KevalaKumbhakaIsWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Retention alone is placed at withdrawn absorption"@en, "Das alleinige Anhalten liegt bei abgezogener Vertiefung"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:KevalaKumbhaka ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situationGround "Retention held at will without the filling and the emptying that ordinarily frame it. A suspension of the breath held as the accomplished form of the practice is the case this coordinate names by its own definition; the practitioner is awake and the ordinary sensory traffic has stopped being the point."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.71-74 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" .

#
# Opened again on 2026-10-22 for the examination of
# pmc:TheWaiverRunsBothWays and pmc:AttainmentRelievesOnlyTheOutwardCondition
# (examinations/the-waiver-runs-both-ways-tested.ttl). Read continuously in the
# Sanskrit with Brahmānanda's Jyotsnā: I.11-17, I.56-64, II.10-14, and III.83-85, which a
# term search for the setting aside of rules reached; the rest
# of the work by whole-text term search only. Everything read in those four
# stretches that falls under the five kinds SPEC §15 owes is entered below,
# whether or not it bears on the examination.
#
# Citation. Verse numbers are those of the Bombay printing with the Jyotsnā,
# which Iyangar's translation follows in the chapters read. Sinh's
# translation numbers the second chapter alike, the third at vajrolī one
# lower, and the first chapter from verse 17 on two higher, because it numbers the two verses on the restraints and observances
# that the Bombay printing sets in quotation marks; each citation gives his
# number as well. The Sanskrit is cited without page: the running heads of the
# text layer are not monotonic.
#

pmc:HathaGopya a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "The knowledge of haṭha is to be kept secret: kept secret it has force, made known it has none"@en, "Das Wissen vom Haṭha ist geheim zu halten: geheim hat es Kraft, bekannt gemacht keine"@de ;
    skos:definition "The yogi who wants accomplishment is to keep the knowledge of haṭha supremely secret, for kept secret it is potent and made known it is impotent. The Jyotsnā gloss adds the qualifications of the one to whom it may be given, from Yājñavalkya and other authorities: with senses conquered, calm, free of attachment, keeping the restraints and observances, devoted to his teacher; and that it is not to be given to one who wears only the dress."@en ;
    skos:note "A sanction on the practice and not on the person: what disclosure costs is the force of the knowledge itself. The book that says so is a manual in four chapters written out for readers, and Iyangar draws the consequence in his note to the verse, that the decisive processes must then be learned from a teacher and that whoever practises from the book alone will come to harm; the note is his and not the verse's."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HathaAbhyasakrama ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.11, Sanskrit with the Jyotsnā of Brahmānanda (Bombay: Tattvavivecaka Press, printed for Tukārām Tātyā, Śaka 1814)" ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, The Hatha Yoga Pradipika, trans. Pancham Sinh (Allahabad: Sudhindra Nath Vasu, Bhuvaneshwari Ashrama; year not legible in the text layer), I.11" ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Hatha-yoga-pradipika, trans. Srinivasa Iyangar, 3rd ed. (Adyar: Theosophical Publishing House, 1949; first ed. 1893), I.11, p. 5" .

pmc:HathaYogamatha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "The hermitage of the haṭha yogi: a place built for the purpose, in a country of a stated kind"@en, "Die Klause des Haṭha-Yogin: ein eigens gebauter Ort in einem Land bestimmter Art"@de ;
    skos:definition "The practitioner is to stay alone in a small hermitage, in a well-governed and righteous country with plentiful alms and free of disturbance, with no rock, fire or water within a bow's length. The hermitage has a small door, no window, hole or pit, is neither too high nor too long, is well smeared with cow-dung and free of insects, and has outside it a pavilion, a raised platform and a well, the whole enclosed by a wall; seated there and free of every care, he is to practise yoga in the way the teacher has shown."@en ;
    skos:note "The one requirement of the manual that is fixed outside the practitioner in the way a built place is, and the one the examination asked about first. Neither the verse nor Brahmānanda's gloss releases anybody from it: the gloss explains each feature by its use — the righteous country for suitable food, the solitude against quarrels, the hut against cold and heat — and quotes the Nandikeśvara Purāṇa for further features. A whole-text search of the Sanskrit for the terms of absence and incapacity found no release of the hermitage anywhere in the work (pmp:WaiverHathapradipikaMathaSearch)."@en ;
    skos:note "The gloss on I.12 says that a half-verse some copies carry with the hermitage verse, on success through suitable food and recreation, was put in by someone else and is not explained, and states its rule: verses of the Pradīpikā that it does not explain are all to be taken as interpolated. The two verses on the ten restraints and ten observances, which the Bombay printing sets in quotation marks after I.16 and which Sinh numbers 17 and 18, are not explained by it and are interpolations by that rule."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.12-14, Sanskrit with the Jyotsnā of Brahmānanda (Bombay: Tattvavivecaka Press, printed for Tukārām Tātyā, Śaka 1814)" ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, The Hatha Yoga Pradipika, trans. Pancham Sinh (Allahabad: Sudhindra Nath Vasu, Bhuvaneshwari Ashrama; year not legible in the text layer), I.12-14" ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Hatha-yoga-pradipika, trans. Srinivasa Iyangar, 3rd ed. (Adyar: Theosophical Publishing House, 1949; first ed. 1893), I.12-14, pp. 6-7" .

pmc:HathaSadbhirYogoVinasyati a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Yoga is destroyed by six things, the holding to vows among them"@en, "Der Yoga wird durch sechs Dinge zerstört, das Festhalten an Gelübden darunter"@de ;
    skos:definition "Overeating, exertion, much talk, the holding to vows, the company of people and restlessness: by these six yoga is destroyed. The Jyotsnā names the vows: bathing in cold water in the morning, eating at night, living on fruit and the like."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted among the conditions of failure of the stretch read, and it is the one the manual names for a practice that comes to nothing: every item on it is a state or an act of the practitioner, none a place, a day or a material. The fourth item turns against the ascetic rule itself. A manual that lists the keeping of vows among the things that destroy the practice has drawn its line between its own regimen and the regimens of others, and the morning bath and the fast return at I.61 as things the beginner is to avoid."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HathaAbhyasakrama ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.15, Sanskrit with the Jyotsnā of Brahmānanda (Bombay: Tattvavivecaka Press, printed for Tukārām Tātyā, Śaka 1814)" ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, The Hatha Yoga Pradipika, trans. Pancham Sinh (Allahabad: Sudhindra Nath Vasu, Bhuvaneshwari Ashrama; year not legible in the text layer), I.15" ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Hatha-yoga-pradipika, trans. Srinivasa Iyangar, 3rd ed. (Adyar: Theosophical Publishing House, 1949; first ed. 1893), I.15, p. 9" .

pmc:HathaSadbhirYogahPrasidhyati a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Yoga succeeds by six things"@en, "Der Yoga gelingt durch sechs Dinge"@de ;
    skos:definition "By zeal, daring, steadfastness, knowledge of the truth, firm conviction and the abandoning of company: by these six yoga succeeds. The Jyotsnā glosses daring as setting to work without first weighing whether the thing can be done, steadfastness as the untiring certainty that it will succeed within his lifetime, and conviction as faith in the words of the scriptures and the teacher."@en ;
    skos:note "The counterpart of the six destroyers in the next verse and stated in the same form: dispositions of the practitioner, nothing outside him. No sign is attached by which the practitioner could tell that he has them."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.16, Sanskrit with the Jyotsnā of Brahmānanda (Bombay: Tattvavivecaka Press, printed for Tukārām Tātyā, Śaka 1814)" ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, The Hatha Yoga Pradipika, trans. Pancham Sinh (Allahabad: Sudhindra Nath Vasu, Bhuvaneshwari Ashrama; year not legible in the text layer), I.16" ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Hatha-yoga-pradipika, trans. Srinivasa Iyangar, 3rd ed. (Adyar: Theosophical Publishing House, 1949; first ed. 1893), I.16, p. 9" .

pmp:HathaAbhyasakrama a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "The course of haṭha practice: posture, retention, seal, attention to the inner sound"@en, "Der Übungsgang des Haṭha: Haltung, Anhalten, Siegel, Lauschen auf den inneren Klang"@de ;
    skos:definition "The order of practice the first chapter closes on: posture, the various retentions, the practice called seal, and then attention to the inner sound, with the purification of the channels and the breath practices of the seals before them; the Jyotsnā calls the last the sequence of practice in haṭha."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.56-57, Sanskrit with the Jyotsnā of Brahmānanda (Bombay: Tattvavivecaka Press, printed for Tukārām Tātyā, Śaka 1814)" ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, The Hatha Yoga Pradipika, trans. Pancham Sinh (Allahabad: Sudhindra Nath Vasu, Bhuvaneshwari Ashrama; year not legible in the text layer), I.58-59" ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Hatha-yoga-pradipika, trans. Srinivasa Iyangar, 3rd ed. (Adyar: Theosophical Publishing House, 1949; first ed. 1893), I.56-57, pp. 24-25" .

pmc:HathaSiddhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "Accomplishment in haṭha"@en, "Vollendung im Haṭha"@de ;
    skos:definition "Being accomplished, siddha, in haṭha, which the first chapter promises after a year to the practitioner who keeps its conditions. The verse does not say what the accomplished one can do; the Jyotsnā glosses siddha as accomplished in haṭha, siddhahaṭha, and says the verse states the limit of time for it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.57, Sanskrit with the Jyotsnā of Brahmānanda (Bombay: Tattvavivecaka Press, printed for Tukārām Tātyā, Śaka 1814)" .

pmc:HathaAbdadUrdhvamSiddhi a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Hathapradipika ;
    rdfs:label "After a year he becomes accomplished; there is no room for doubt"@en, "Nach einem Jahr wird er vollendet; daran ist nicht zu zweifeln"@de ;
    skos:definition "The yogi who is continent, keeps the measured diet, renounces and is devoted to yoga, and follows the course of practice to the attention to the inner sound, becomes accomplished after a year, and no deliberation is to be had about it. The Jyotsnā: after a year, varṣād ūrdhvam, he becomes accomplished in haṭha; the doubt whether it will be so or not is not to be raised; this is certain."@en ;
    skos:note "A term. It is stated with four conditions on the practitioner and a word of certainty, and it names no sign by which the accomplishment at the end of the year would be known and no consequence if it does not come; a practitioner in whom nothing has happened after a year is left, by this verse, with the four conditions to examine in himself."@en ;
    skos:note "The two translations read the term differently, and only one of them as the Sanskrit has it. Abdād ūrdhvam is after or beyond a year, and the commentary says the same. Iyangar renders within more than a year; Sinh renders gains success, no doubt, within a year, which turns a floor into a ceiling. A reader working from Sinh alone would take the verse to promise accomplishment before a year is out."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HathaAbhyasakrama ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HathaSiddhi ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.57, Sanskrit with the Jyotsnā of Brahmānanda (Bombay: Tattvavivecaka Press, printed for Tukārām Tātyā, Śaka 1814)" ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, The Hatha Yoga Pradipika, trans. Pancham Sinh (Allahabad: Sudhindra Nath Vasu, Bhuvaneshwari Ashrama; year not legible in the text layer), I.59" ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Hatha-yoga-pradipika, trans. Srinivasa Iyangar, 3rd ed. (Adyar: Theosophical Publishing House, 1949; first ed. 1893), I.57, pp. 24-25" .

